# Bookings

> Dated occurrences with a fixed number of identical places, a hold
> that cannot oversell, and the money schedule for each booking.

**Requires:** the `core` and `auth` engines. The guest is an
`Auth::Identity` and must be signed in before holding; `bookings.identity_id`
stores that id as a bare bigint with no association and no database
foreign key. Bookings never joins to `auth_identities`.

**Does not require:** billing. Nothing in this engine names a Billing
constant. Card payment arrives as a later release that listens to the
events below.

## Model

| Model | Table | Owns |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Bookings::Offering` | `booking_offerings` | name, slug, host-defined `kind`, `confirmation` (`automatic` or `manual`), `status` (`draft`, `published`) |
| `Bookings::Occurrence` | `booking_occurrences` | `starts_at`, `ends_at`, `capacity`, `price_cents`, `deposit_cents` (null means pay in full), `currency`, `details` jsonb, `status` (`draft`, `published`, `cancelled`) |
| `Bookings::Booking` | `bookings` | `identity_id`, `quantity`, `status`, `expires_at`, public `reference`, optional `reason`, opaque `customer_ref` |
| `Bookings::Instalment` | `booking_instalments` | `kind` (`deposit`, `balance`, `full`), `amount_cents`, `due_on`, `state` (`due`, `requested`, `paid`, `refunded`), unique `payable_ref`, `checkout_url` |

One generated app is one operator. The sellable unit is one occurrence
with N identical places. Price and currency live on the occurrence.

## The lock

The occurrence row is the lock. `Bookings::HoldService` takes it with
`with_lock`, sums `quantity` over bookings that are `held`,
`pending_review`, or `confirmed`, inserts the booking as `held` and its
first instalment (the deposit, or the full price), and commits.
`rejected`, `cancelled`, and `expired` do not count.

The capacity check lives on `Bookings::Booking` itself, so a walk-in
created from the console and an Administrate form take the same lock.
Lowering `capacity` on an occurrence takes it too; a cut below the
places already taken is refused.

Booking statuses: `held`, `pending_review`, `confirmed`, `rejected`,
`cancelled`, `expired`.

```ruby
result = Bookings::HoldService.call(occurrence: occurrence, identity_id: current_identity.id, quantity: 2)
result.ok?     # => true when the places were taken
result.value   # => the held Bookings::Booking
result.error   # => "Quantity exceeds the 1 places remaining" when refused
```

## Configuration

```ruby
# config/initializers/bookings.rb
Bookings.configure do |c|
  c.hold_ttl = 30.minutes   # default; the engine refuses anything above 24 hours
end
```

## The sweep

`Bookings::HoldSweepJob` finds every `held` booking past `expires_at`
and publishes `booking.hold_expiring.bookings`. It does not change the
row. The place is freed only when the payment side reports the session
dead, in a later release. Schedule it every minute. With Solid Queue:

```yaml
# config/recurring.yml
production:
  bookings_hold_sweep:
    class: Bookings::HoldSweepJob
    schedule: every minute
```

## Marking an instalment paid by hand

An operator who took a bank transfer or cash records it with
`Bookings::Instalments::MarkPaidService`. It takes the occurrence lock,
marks the instalment `paid`, and, when the instalment is the first
payment and the booking is still `held`, confirms the booking for an
`automatic` offering or moves it to `pending_review` for a `manual`
one. A balance payment marks the instalment paid and leaves the status
alone. Calling it twice is a no-op.

```ruby
Bookings::Instalments::MarkPaidService.call(instalment: booking.first_instalment)
```

## Events emitted

| Event name | Payload | Emitted when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `occurrence.published.bookings` | `{ occurrence_id:, offering_id:, starts_at:, capacity: }` | `Occurrence#publish!` |
| `booking.held.bookings` | `{ booking_id:, reference:, occurrence_id:, offering_id:, identity_id:, quantity:, expires_at:, payable_ref:, amount_cents:, currency:, description: }` | `HoldService` commits a hold |
| `booking.hold_expiring.bookings` | `{ booking_id:, reference:, occurrence_id:, identity_id:, expires_at:, payable_ref: }` | `HoldSweepJob` finds a `held` row past `expires_at` |
| `booking.confirmed.bookings` | `{ booking_id:, reference:, occurrence_id:, identity_id:, quantity: }` | the first payment confirms an `automatic` booking |
| `booking.balance_due.bookings` | registered; emitted by the balance release | |
| `booking.rejected.bookings` | registered; emitted by the review release | |
| `booking.cancelled.bookings` | registered; emitted by the cancel release | |

All events are published after the database transaction commits.

## Events consumed

| Event name | Subscriber | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| _none yet_ | | The payment listeners arrive with the billing release. |

## Exposed concerns

| Concern | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| _none_ | |

## Mounting

```ruby
# config/routes.rb (host application)
Rails.application.routes.draw do
  mount Bookings::Engine, at: "/bookings"
end
```

## Running the specs

```bash
bin/rails seams:test[bookings]
```

The hold service spec races two threads for the last place against a
real Postgres database, so it runs outside the transactional fixture.
